  "output": "## Summary\n\nThe codebase is at an early, honest stage: **`web/` has real, working code** (Server-Component console + Drizzle queries), while **all of `engine/`** — connectors, blocking/matching, detection, the resolution graph, the eval harness — is scaffolding that raises `NotImplementedError`. So this splits into two kinds of findings: concrete fixes in `web/`, and forward-looking guardrails for `engine/` tied to constraints AGENTS.md already calls out (worth fixing *before* you write the first real line, since these patterns are easy to bake in wrong).\n\n---\n\n## 1. N+1 query patterns\n\n**Web layer: none found.** `lib/server/runs.ts` and `lib/server/export.ts` consistently use `innerJoin`/`leftJoin` (e.g. `getRunDetail`, `loadDecisions`) instead of fetching a run then looping to fetch sources/ruleset, and independent reads are correctly parallelized with `Promise.all` (`web/app/(console)/rules/page.tsx:16`, `web/lib/server/export.ts:90`). This is already the right shape — don't regress it when the `conflicts/` and `audit/` pages get built; keep joining rather than fetching a match list and then looping per-conflict for its entities.\n\n**Engine: the real risk hasn't been written yet.** `db.py`'s write functions (`write_entities`, `write_matches`, `write_conflicts`) are currently stubs, but the docstring says a run produces \"thousands of rows.\" A naive implementation — a Python loop doing one `INSERT` per row — is the textbook N+1 at pipeline scale:\n\n```python\n# Don't do this when you implement write_entities:\nfor entity in entities:\n    conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO entities (...) VALUES (...)\", entity.dict())\n```\n\n```python\n# Batch it — one round trip per table per run:\ndef write_entities(run_id: UUID, entities: list[CanonicalEntity]) -> None:\n    with psycopg.connect(DATABASE_URL) as conn, conn.cursor() as cur:\n        with cur.copy(\n            \"COPY entities (id, run_id, source_id, external_id, entity_type, \"\n            \"fields, parent_external_id, last_modified_at) FROM STDIN\"\n        ) as copy:\n            for e in entities:\n                copy.write_row((...))\n```\nUse `COPY` (psycopg3 supports it natively) or at minimum `executemany`/multi-row `INSERT ... VALUES (...), (...), ...` — never a per-row loop for `entities`/`matches`/`conflicts`/`resolutions`.\n\nSame shape applies to **embedding generation** in `pipeline/blocking.py::block()` — batch-encode with `sentence_transformers.encode(list_of_strings)`, don't call `.encode(single_string)` per entity in a loop; that turns O(n) inference calls into O(1) batched ones.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Unnecessary re-renders in React\n\n**Also mostly clean** — the console is Server Components by design, and there's exactly one client subtree: `RunLive` → `RunProgress` (polling) + `Elapsed` (clock). Nothing else re-renders on an interval.\n\nOne real thing to fix now, before it compounds: `RunProgress` (`web/components/runs/run-live.tsx:43`) re-renders every 2s (poll) and re-renders `<StatGrid>` and `<RunStageBar>` as new elements each time, even when the underlying `status`/`stats` are unchanged (e.g. two consecutive polls both return `\"matching\"`). Cheap today because these are small pure components, but if `StatGrid`/`RunStageBar` grow (they're prime candidates once the conflict queue exists), wrap them:\n\n```tsx\n// stat-grid.tsx / run-stage-bar.tsx\nexport const StatGrid = memo(function StatGrid({ stats, status, variant, className }: Props) {\n  /* ... */\n});\n```\n`memo` on these two is a no-cost, high-leverage change because their props are plain data (no unstable callbacks/objects passed in), so shallow comparison actually works.\n\nNo other re-render issues: `RulesetEditor` and `NewRunForm` correctly use `useMemo` for derived filtered lists (`web/components/runs/new-run-form.tsx:28-35`), and state updates use functional `setRules((current) => ...)` rather than closing over stale state.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities\n\n**a) `countSourcesBySide` does a full scan + JS filter instead of an aggregate** (`web/lib/server/sources.ts:24-31`):\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();          // fetches every row\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\nPush the grouping into SQL — cheap at today's row counts, but it's the wrong instinct to carry into a table that grows (and it's needless work on every `get-started` page load):\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, total: count() })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n\n  const by = Object.fromEntries(rows.map((r) => [r.kind, r.total]));\n  return {\n    crm: (by.salesforce ?? 0) + (by.hubspot ?? 0),\n    warehouse: (by.databricks ?? 0) + (by.snowflake ?? 0),\n    synthetic: by.synthetic ?? 0,\n  };\n}\n```\n\n**b) The Anthropic prompt-caching lever AGENTS.md specifies isn't wired up yet** — `agent/graph.py::resolve()` is a stub, but the doc is explicit: *\"Enable prompt caching on the ruleset block — it is identical across every call in a run and is the single largest cost lever.\"* When you implement `resolve()`, mark the ruleset block with `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}` in the system prompt so it's not re-sent/re-billed per conflict:\n\n```python\nresponse = client.messages.create(\n    model=\"claude-sonnet-5\",\n    system=[\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": build_ruleset_block(rules),\n         \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}},   # reused across every conflict in the run\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": PROPOSE_INSTRUCTIONS},\n    ],\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": conflict_prompt(conflict)}],\n)\n```\n\n**c) No request-level dedup on `lib/server/*` reads.** Every Server Component call to `listSources()`/`getRunDetail()` is a fresh DB round trip; there's no shared React `cache()` wrapper. Not a bug today because each page currently calls each query once, but the moment two components in the same request tree need the same data (e.g. a future `conflicts/` page reading `getRunDetail` in both a layout and the page), you'll get a duplicate query unless it's wrapped:\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\nexport const getRunDetail = cache(async (runId: string): Promise<RunDetail | null> => { /* ... */ });\n```\nWorth doing preemptively on `getRunDetail` and `listSources` specifically, since AGENTS.md's Phase 4 plan (`conflicts/`, `audit/`) will likely reuse them.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Memory leaks\n\n**None found in the current code** — this is worth stating plainly rather than manufacturing a finding:\n- `Elapsed`'s `setInterval` is cleaned up correctly (`web/components/runs/run-live.tsx:106-107`).\n- `QueryProvider` creates one `QueryClient` per mount via `useState(() => ...)`, scoped to `RunLive`'s lifetime, not the root layout — garbage collected on navigation away, no client-side cache growing unbounded across the whole app session.\n- `lib/db/index.ts` correctly singletons the Postgres connection on `globalThis` specifically to avoid HMR-driven connection-pool leaks in dev (`web/lib/db/index.ts:19-24`) — this is the right pattern, not a bug.\n- `engine.ts`'s two `fetch` calls both set `AbortSignal.timeout(...)`, so a hung engine can't leak an open request indefinitely.\n\n**One forward-looking risk**, since it's structural: `main.py::start_run` dispatches `execute_run` via FastAPI's `BackgroundTasks` (`engine/main.py:38`). Starlette runs sync background tasks in its thread pool, so it won't block the event loop outright, but that pool has a small fixed capacity — a few concurrent, CPU-bound runs (embedding generation, matching) will saturate it and start starving the `/runs/{id}/status` polling endpoint the console depends on. When you implement `execute_run`, either keep the pipeline stages non-blocking/chunked, or move execution to a real worker (RQ/Celery/separate process) rather than relying on `BackgroundTasks` past the single-run dev case.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Redundant computations\n\n- **`countSourcesBySide`** — covered above (§3a); it's redundant computation *and* a caching miss in one.\n- **`RulesetEditor`** recomputes `orderRulesByEvaluation(rules)` and the `sameRules(rules, active.rules)` dirty-check on every keystroke (`web/components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:61,65`). At realistic ruleset sizes (tens of rules) this is O(n log n) + O(n) per render, which is trivially cheap — flagging only so it doesn't get copied into a component that renders hundreds of rows (the future conflict queue is the obvious place this pattern would actually cost something; wrap that one in `useMemo` when it's built).\n- **Engine, forward-looking:** `pipeline/detect.py::detect()` takes `entities: dict[UUID, CanonicalEntity]` as a parameter (`engine/pipeline/detect.py:40`) — good, that signature already implies \"load once, diff in memory,\" not a per-match DB lookup. Keep that shape when you implement it; it's the difference between one `SELECT` and thousands.\n\n---\n\n### Bottom line\nThe web console is already doing the disciplined thing (joins over loops, `Promise.all` over sequential awaits, Server Components over client fetching) — the two real fixes there are the `countSourcesBySide` aggregate query and `memo`-ing `StatGrid`/`RunStageBar`. The bigger performance decisions all live ahead of you in `engine/`: batch every write in `db.py`, batch embedding calls in `blocking.py`, and wire up the prompt-caching AGENTS.md already specifies when `agent/graph.py::resolve()` gets built.\n",
